#{[17592186172929 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172929 #edb/ref 1061 32465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172929 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b76b9e7-6d5f-584c-837f-bd003bd3c835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172929 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173163 #edb/ref 1064 "A  plane curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r:[a,b]\\to \\Bbb R^2\n$$\n\nis said to be **simple** if it doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints. Notice that  this means that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t_1)\\neq \\mathbf r(t_2), \\qquad \na < t_1< t_2 < b.\n$$\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is simple while curve II is not simple.\n\n![](images/7b/7be1bc23d37c187a642d51917d6acb846147059bc6e9978d6ccd4ed35eed8794.png)\n\nA  plane curve is said to be **closed** if its terminal point coincides with its initial point, that is, if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(b)= \\mathbf r(a).\n$$\n\n![](images/4b/4b21a6e638cb8b0bae8967ba142fbfacb14349f34ff80d9359643e03ea1c1f83.png)\n\nFor example, curve III shown below is closed, while curve IV is not:\n\n![](images/85/8507fb039da42f37307ca3994cfb5ad66b288fb28d0cfd1a1a179acd2f83ae5e.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Curve III is an example of both a closed and simple curve. Notice that an example of a closed but *not* simple curve looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86dcd1dc864a2f0e2ceade072ba230b8fb4fe8923189a7eed2964f6f66a67651.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173163 #edb/ref 1065 5100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173163 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "72ccdc89-4807-5abb-9f69-7e017f195c3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173163 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173171 #edb/ref 1064 "A very important (although very hard to prove) result in mathematics states the following:\n\nAny simple and closed curve divides the plane into exactly two regions, one inside the curve and one outside.\n\nBased on this fact, we can define the **orientation** of a simple closed curve as follows:\n\nA simple closed curve is **positively oriented** if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is **negatively oriented**.\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is positively oriented, while curve II is negatively oriented:\n\n![](images/95/9527dd8bdfd979a1d521ccf945e84ef9b557d67e3d89ce8f5e4f7bb8d287ee8c.png)\n\nAn alternative convenient way of looking at simple oriented curves is that a curve is positively oriented if it is traversed *counterclockwise* and it is negatively oriented if it is traversed *clockwise*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173171 #edb/ref 1065 5165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173171 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c2c2591-a914-53a3-9f60-6e7335d049f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173171 #edb/ref 1067 "Oriented Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186192957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Probability That a Random Variable Lies Within a Bounded Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192753 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fe4b690c-94a9-5a27-b0e8-5583ba6dba19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192788 #edb/ref 103